1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:13,785
There are two kinds of people in an organisation, and
Albert Wesker had spent four months

2
00:00:13,805 --> 00:00:15,810
working with only one of them.

3
00:00:16,210 --> 00:00:21,591
The Absolute did whatever he said. They did it
perfectly, instantly and without a flicker,

4
00:00:21,611 --> 00:00:26,873
and by the third week of January he had established
the shape of their limitation, which

5
00:00:26,893 --> 00:00:32,095
was this: they never once surprised him. Ask Hale to
secure a building and the building

6
00:00:32,115 --> 00:00:37,497
was secure. Ask Hale how it might be attacked and he
would give you a competent, complete,

7
00:00:37,517 --> 00:00:42,599
entirely unimaginative list. Voss would run any
experiment he designed. She would not

8
00:00:42,619 --> 00:00:43,800
design a better one.

9
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:50,465
A company of such people can execute anything. It
cannot *invent* anything. And so, in

10
00:00:50,485 --> 00:00:56,896
the third week of January, Wesker went shopping for
the other kind — the kind that could

11
00:00:56,916 --> 00:00:57,335
leave.

12
00:00:57,734 --> 00:01:01,104
He found the first one in a paper
nobody had read.

13
00:01:01,505 --> 00:01:07,749
*Marsh, E. — Serotype-independent persistence of
adenoviral vectors in primate airway

14
00:01:07,769 --> 00:01:14,897
epithelium: a failure analysis.* Forty-one citations, all
of them by people politely disagreeing.

15
00:01:14,917 --> 00:01:21,235
Published eleven months ago and buried, because it
was a paper about why something had

16
00:01:21,255 --> 00:01:25,215
not worked, and nobody gets a
professorship for those.

17
00:01:25,614 --> 00:01:31,284
Wesker read it twice on a Sunday and drove to
Philadelphia on a Tuesday.

18
00:01:31,685 --> 00:01:37,867
Dr. Evelyn Marsh was twenty-seven, four years into a
postdoctoral position that had two

19
00:01:37,887 --> 00:01:44,569
months left in it, and alone in the lab at ten o'clock
at night, which was where she generally

20
00:01:44,589 --> 00:01:44,855
was.

21
00:01:45,375 --> 00:01:49,085
We're closed — I mean, the building's closed,
you need a card, how did you —

22
00:01:49,484 --> 00:01:51,695
Your negative controls are wrong.

23
00:01:52,094 --> 00:01:53,125
That stopped her.

24
00:01:53,644 --> 00:01:54,334
I'm sorry?

25
00:01:54,734 --> 00:02:00,047
Figure four. You ran your uninfected controls in the
same incubator as your infected plates,

26
00:02:00,067 --> 00:02:05,147
on the same day, on a shared gas line. Your control
wells are not clean. They are simply

27
00:02:05,167 --> 00:02:05,785
less dirty.

28
00:02:06,185 --> 00:02:08,895
That's — the lab's protocol,
everyone runs —

29
00:02:09,295 --> 00:02:13,875
Everyone is wrong, which is why your figure four
looks like noise, which is why three

30
00:02:13,895 --> 00:02:18,854
reviewers told you that your effect was not real. It is
real. You have simply been measuring

31
00:02:18,874 --> 00:02:20,585
it against a contaminated floor.

32
00:02:20,985 --> 00:02:26,326
She stood there with a pipette in her hand, in a lab
coat that had somebody else's name

33
00:02:26,346 --> 00:02:31,687
stitched on it because hers had never come, looking
at a man in black sunglasses at ten

34
00:02:31,707 --> 00:02:33,536
at night in a locked building.

35
00:02:34,056 --> 00:02:34,836
Who are you?

36
00:02:35,235 --> 00:02:37,045
Look at figure six.

37
00:02:37,445 --> 00:02:38,895
She looked at figure six.

38
00:02:39,415 --> 00:02:43,930
Your vectors cleared at twenty-eight days in four
animals and persisted past ninety in

39
00:02:43,950 --> 00:02:48,569
two. You reported a mean. You buried the most
interesting result in your own paper under

40
00:02:48,589 --> 00:02:51,786
an average, because a mean is what your
supervisor asked for.

41
00:02:52,186 --> 00:02:53,628
The two that persisted were —

42
00:02:54,028 --> 00:02:59,050
Both female. Both from the second cohort. Both, if I
have read your supplementary table

43
00:02:59,070 --> 00:03:02,758
correctly, housed separately after a
ventilation fault in March.

44
00:03:03,433 --> 00:03:04,223
Oh my God.

45
00:03:04,623 --> 00:03:10,142
Yes. You did not discover a failure, Dr. Marsh. You
discovered that persistence is conditional,

46
00:03:10,162 --> 00:03:13,173
and then you apologised for it for
thirty-one pages.

47
00:03:13,574 --> 00:03:19,952
The narrator would like to record that this
conversation lasted ninety seconds, that Albert

48
00:03:19,972 --> 00:03:26,070
Wesker had prepared for it for six hours, and that
he had not said one single word that

49
00:03:26,090 --> 00:03:26,843
was untrue.

50
00:03:27,243 --> 00:03:32,498
That is the whole trick. He never needed to lie to
a clever person. He needed only to

51
00:03:32,518 --> 00:03:36,593
be the first human being in their life who
had read them properly.

52
00:03:37,114 --> 00:03:38,043
What do you want?

53
00:03:38,443 --> 00:03:43,535
I have a facility in Wilmington with a BSL-4 sublevel, a
freezer archive nobody has catalogued,

54
00:03:43,555 --> 00:03:48,269
and no one in it who can think. Your contract expires
on the thirty-first of March. Come

55
00:03:48,289 --> 00:03:53,004
on the first of February. You will report to me. You
will not write grants. You will not

56
00:03:53,024 --> 00:03:56,393
teach. You will not explain yourself to
a committee ever again.

57
00:03:56,794 --> 00:03:57,644
What's the salary?

58
00:03:58,044 --> 00:04:02,296
Whatever you ask for. It is the least interesting
question you could have asked and I

59
00:04:02,316 --> 00:04:03,753
will forgive you for it once.

60
00:04:04,153 --> 00:04:09,612
She came on the first of February. She gave four
weeks' notice the next morning and spent

61
00:04:09,632 --> 00:04:11,643
the last of them barely sleeping.

62
00:04:12,794 --> 00:04:20,931
Roster. New. Doctor Evelyn Marsh. Human. Loyalty,
sixty-one per cent. Human loyalty is

63
00:04:20,951 --> 00:04:28,994
earned, and is visible to you at all times.
Consider what you are willing to spend to

64
00:04:29,014 --> 00:04:29,848
raise it.

65
00:04:30,437 --> 00:04:32,848
I am willing to spend nothing at all.

66
00:04:33,247 --> 00:04:34,117
Noted.

67
00:04:34,517 --> 00:04:39,138
You misunderstand. I do not intend to *buy* her.
Money buys a floor, not a ceiling. She

68
00:04:39,158 --> 00:04:43,779
has spent four years being the cleverest person in
every room she has entered and being

69
00:04:43,799 --> 00:04:48,314
told to wait her turn. I am going to give her work
that frightens her and an audience

70
00:04:48,334 --> 00:04:50,288
of one that is impossible to impress.

71
00:04:50,687 --> 00:04:52,161
And that will be enough?

72
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:57,196
That will be *everything*. You have the wrong model
of people, System. They do not follow

73
00:04:57,216 --> 00:05:01,798
what pays them. They follow whatever makes them feel
like the version of themselves they

74
00:05:01,818 --> 00:05:03,211
always suspected they were.

75
00:05:03,610 --> 00:05:05,620
The second one came to destroy him.

76
00:05:06,021 --> 00:05:12,088
Catherine Adeyemi was thirty-eight, British,
twenty-seven years in London private equity

77
00:05:12,108 --> 00:05:18,106
and two in a fund that had recently taken an
interest in American diagnostics. She flew

78
00:05:18,126 --> 00:05:24,193
into Philadelphia on the third of February with a
folder of numbers and a firm intention

79
00:05:24,213 --> 00:05:28,550
to build a short position, because the
numbers were impossible.

80
00:05:28,951 --> 00:05:34,958
They were, in fact, impossible. That was the trouble
with everything the System did. The

81
00:05:34,978 --> 00:05:41,260
paperwork was perfect, the history was perfect, the
witnesses were perfect — and the *rates*

82
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:47,356
were wrong. A company does not go from nothing to
four hundred and six placed instruments

83
00:05:47,376 --> 00:05:53,315
in ninety days. A plant does not appear in a filing
with fourteen years of depreciation

84
00:05:53,335 --> 00:05:59,206
and no capital expenditure to match. Every
individual document was flawless. The shape

85
00:05:59,226 --> 00:06:04,481
they made together was a lie, and Catherine
Adeyemi read shapes for a living.

86
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:10,666
She came to the building expecting to be stonewalled
by a general counsel. She was shown

87
00:06:10,686 --> 00:06:16,339
into the boardroom and given coffee, and twelve
minutes later Wesker came in alone and

88
00:06:16,359 --> 00:06:17,790
sat down opposite her.

89
00:06:18,310 --> 00:06:23,986
Dr. Wesker. I'll be direct. I think your accounts
are fraudulent and I intend to say so

90
00:06:24,006 --> 00:06:24,641
in public.

91
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:26,110
Which line?

92
00:06:26,511 --> 00:06:30,952
The Newark plant. Two hundred and eighty thousand
square feet, booked at a carrying value

93
00:06:30,972 --> 00:06:35,816
of thirty-one million with accumulated depreciation from
2011, and I cannot find the acquisition.

94
00:06:35,836 --> 00:06:40,177
I have the county recorder. I have fourteen years of
filings. I have your predecessor's

95
00:06:40,197 --> 00:06:44,589
accounts, which I obtained legally, and there is no
plant in them, and then in the third

96
00:06:44,609 --> 00:06:47,347
quarter there is a plant, and it has
always been there.

97
00:06:47,747 --> 00:06:53,797
Wesker did not blink. He had known this question
would arrive; he had merely expected

98
00:06:53,817 --> 00:06:55,297
it from a government.

99
00:06:55,817 --> 00:06:57,027
You are correct.

100
00:06:57,427 --> 00:06:58,497
I'm sorry?

101
00:06:58,896 --> 00:07:03,697
You are correct that it cannot be accounted for. You
are wrong about the conclusion. Fraud

102
00:07:03,716 --> 00:07:08,356
is a claim about value, Ms. Adeyemi. Go to Route 72.
Stand in the truck yard. Count the

103
00:07:08,376 --> 00:07:13,015
vehicles. Watch a cartridge come off the line and
put it in your handbag and take it to

104
00:07:13,035 --> 00:07:17,621
any laboratory you like. The plant is *there*. It
works. It made nine hundred thousand

105
00:07:17,641 --> 00:07:22,387
cartridges last month. Whatever you believe about
where it came from, there is no version

106
00:07:22,407 --> 00:07:25,387
of the story in which shorting my
stock makes you money.

107
00:07:25,786 --> 00:07:27,656
She sat very still for a moment.

108
00:07:28,177 --> 00:07:29,447
Where did it come from?

109
00:07:29,846 --> 00:07:35,076
That is the one question I will not answer today.
Ask me again in five years and I may.

110
00:07:35,476 --> 00:07:41,322
And then he did the thing that neither the lawyers
nor the System nor Adolf would have

111
00:07:41,342 --> 00:07:47,188
advised, and that is the reason Catherine Adeyemi
worked for him for the next fourteen

112
00:07:47,208 --> 00:07:47,597
years.

113
00:07:48,119 --> 00:07:52,427
You are going to publish. You have four hundred
hours of work and a thesis and you are

114
00:07:52,447 --> 00:07:57,108
not a coward, so you will publish, and it will be
wrong, and it will cost you your reputation

115
00:07:57,128 --> 00:08:01,538
— because in six weeks I will report a quarter that
makes your note look like a tantrum.

116
00:08:01,557 --> 00:08:01,689
Or.

117
00:08:02,088 --> 00:08:02,548
Or.

118
00:08:02,947 --> 00:08:07,345
Or you take the chair at the end of this table.
Chief Financial Officer. You build the

119
00:08:07,365 --> 00:08:12,021
public company: the listing, the controls, the
auditors, all of it, properly, to a standard

120
00:08:12,041 --> 00:08:16,593
nobody can attack. And you argue with me about money
whenever you like — loudly, in front

121
00:08:16,613 --> 00:08:19,317
of others, and you will win perhaps
one time in four.

122
00:08:19,716 --> 00:08:22,278
And the other things I might want
to argue about?

123
00:08:22,677 --> 00:08:25,268
You will not argue with me about those.

124
00:08:25,668 --> 00:08:32,402
The pause that followed was eleven seconds long. The
narrator has counted it on the recording

125
00:08:32,422 --> 00:08:38,358
that Hale made, which Wesker knew was being made,
which is a detail worth keeping.

126
00:08:38,878 --> 00:08:41,434
That's the most honest offer I've
ever been made.

127
00:08:41,833 --> 00:08:46,677
It is the only honest offer you have ever been made.
Everyone else told you that you would

128
00:08:46,697 --> 00:08:47,379
be a partner.

129
00:08:48,409 --> 00:08:57,230
Roster. New. Catherine Adeyemi. Human. Loyalty,
twenty-two per cent. Note: this one will

130
00:08:57,250 --> 00:09:00,545
never reach one hundred per cent.

131
00:09:01,135 --> 00:09:06,269
I should hope not. I have twenty-two people who
cannot say no. I have precisely one who

132
00:09:06,289 --> 00:09:06,505
can.

133
00:09:06,905 --> 00:09:12,435
Evelyn Marsh was cleared to the fourth floor. She
was not cleared to the fourth button.

134
00:09:12,836 --> 00:09:18,360
She found out about it in the way that everybody in
that building eventually found out

135
00:09:18,380 --> 00:09:23,839
about everything, which is to say she noticed
something and was told nothing and drew

136
00:09:23,859 --> 00:09:29,706
her own conclusions, and her own conclusions were more
flattering to Wesker than the truth.

137
00:09:30,105 --> 00:09:36,698
What she was given instead was the archive: forty-one
vials marked *ODD — DO NOT DISCARD*,

138
00:09:36,718 --> 00:09:40,885
thirty years of collected accidents,
and one instruction.

139
00:09:41,405 --> 00:09:46,181
Find out what is in them. You will have no deadline,
no oversight and no obligation to

140
00:09:46,201 --> 00:09:50,976
publish. If anything in that freezer is interesting,
I want to know before anyone else

141
00:09:50,996 --> 00:09:52,035
on the planet does.

142
00:09:52,436 --> 00:09:54,673
That's not a project. That's a dream.

143
00:09:55,072 --> 00:09:59,624
It is a test. I do not know what is in those vials
either. When you tell me, I will learn

144
00:09:59,644 --> 00:10:03,323
two things — what is in them, and whether you
are worth the floor space.

145
00:10:03,722 --> 00:10:09,119
She worked ninety hours that week. She did not tell
anyone that she had gone back at two

146
00:10:09,139 --> 00:10:14,597
in the morning on the Saturday, or that when she
found the first thing — a cell line from

147
00:10:14,617 --> 00:10:20,014
2004 that should have senesced in forty passages and
had reached three hundred — she sat

148
00:10:20,034 --> 00:10:25,492
in the cold room and cried for a minute and a half,
and then went and found a whiteboard.

149
00:10:26,643 --> 00:10:35,600
Loyalty. Doctor Evelyn Marsh: sixty-one per cent, to
seventy-eight. Cost to you: nothing.

150
00:10:36,190 --> 00:10:37,920
In the silence, the passenger.

151
00:10:38,440 --> 00:10:39,756
You are good at this.

152
00:10:40,157 --> 00:10:44,047
I am adequate at this. You would have given
her a banner and a title.

153
00:10:44,447 --> 00:10:46,509
And she would have wept for that too.

154
00:10:46,909 --> 00:10:51,062
For a year. Then she would have wanted a bigger
title, and you would have had to give

155
00:10:51,082 --> 00:10:55,334
it to her, and in five years you would have had a
woman with a title and no results and

156
00:10:55,354 --> 00:10:59,654
no way to remove her without insulting the banner.
This way she has nothing but the work

157
00:10:59,674 --> 00:11:02,599
and my attention, and I can withdraw
either one at any time.

158
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:08,825
The narrator notes that this is the cruellest thing
said aloud in this chapter, and that

159
00:11:08,845 --> 00:11:13,210
it was said calmly, in a lift, by a man
who genuinely admired her.

160
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:22,727
Abilities. Command Presence, level one: five
thousand. In any room you address, attention

161
00:11:22,747 --> 00:11:28,288
settles and does not wander. Doubt is
delayed, not removed.

162
00:11:28,878 --> 00:11:33,932
He bought it on the twenty-eighth, out of a balance
that had climbed back to fourteen

163
00:11:33,952 --> 00:11:39,305
thousand eight hundred on the strength of two
recruitments, a quarter, and what the System

164
00:11:39,325 --> 00:11:42,888
called *the acquisition of instruments
that can refuse you.*

165
00:11:43,288 --> 00:11:48,745
It was, he noted, the first thing he had ever
purchased that improved him rather than

166
00:11:48,765 --> 00:11:54,481
his company, and it was also an admission — the first
of very few — that there was a part

167
00:11:54,501 --> 00:11:57,253
of this that he could not
simply out-think.

168
00:11:58,403 --> 00:12:05,947
Purchase. Command Presence, level one. Balance, nine
thousand eight hundred. Presence:

169
00:12:05,967 --> 00:12:12,633
sixteen to twenty-one. This raises the floor.
It does not raise the ceiling.

170
00:12:13,224 --> 00:12:15,354
The voice inside him, unasked.

171
00:12:15,874 --> 00:12:17,917
You bought what I could have taught you.

172
00:12:18,317 --> 00:12:22,878
I bought five points. You will have to supply the
rest, and you will do it when I ask

173
00:12:22,898 --> 00:12:23,687
and not before.

174
00:12:24,086 --> 00:12:25,639
And if I refuse?

175
00:12:26,038 --> 00:12:30,498
Then you will spend the next sixty years watching
me do it badly, which I believe you

176
00:12:30,518 --> 00:12:32,449
would find more painful than I would.

177
00:12:32,849 --> 00:12:36,539
There was no answer to that. There was
rarely an answer to that.

178
00:12:36,938 --> 00:12:42,359
At the end of the first week of March, the two of
them ended up at the same lift at the

179
00:12:42,379 --> 00:12:47,799
same time: the postdoc who had been rescued and the
banker who had come to destroy him.

180
00:12:48,318 --> 00:12:50,538
You've been here a month. What do
you make of him?

181
00:12:50,938 --> 00:12:55,025
He's the only person I've ever met who
isn't wasting my time.

182
00:12:55,426 --> 00:12:56,976
That isn't what I asked.

183
00:12:57,375 --> 00:12:58,365
No. I know.

184
00:12:58,765 --> 00:13:00,495
The lift came. They got in.

185
00:13:01,015 --> 00:13:05,961
He is the most dangerous person who has ever paid me,
and I have been paid by some genuinely

186
00:13:05,981 --> 00:13:06,716
appalling men.

187
00:13:07,116 --> 00:13:07,667
Yes.

188
00:13:08,067 --> 00:13:13,340
And the way she said it — the narrator would like
this noted, because it is the first

189
00:13:13,360 --> 00:13:18,759
time it appears in the record, and it will appear
again and again for sixty-five years,

190
00:13:18,779 --> 00:13:21,873
in a hundred languages, from
millions of throats —

191
00:13:22,273 --> 00:13:24,562
the way she said it was not agreement.

192
00:13:24,962 --> 00:13:26,242
It was gratitude.
